# @boundary runs
# @boundary-pending BUG-234  reverse() byte-reverses, corrupting multi-byte UTF-8
#
# PINS A BUG, NOT A FEATURE. `"世界".reverse()` should be `"界世"`. It is instead a
# BYTE reversal, which shreds every multi-byte codepoint: the result is not merely
# the wrong text, it is **not text at all** — `isValidUtf8()` is false and
# `codePointCount()` is 0, from input that was perfectly valid UTF-8.
#
# The .expected file therefore records BROKEN behaviour on purpose. When BUG-234 is
# fixed this probe will FAIL — rewrite it to assert `cjkRevValid=true`,
# `cjkRevCps=2`, `cjkRev=[界世]`, and drop the pending line.
#
# Found 2026-07-30 by the A3 non-ASCII dimension, from a row written specifically
# because a byte reverse and a codepoint reverse differ exactly here. ASCII is
# unaffected, which is why nothing noticed: `"abc".reverse()` is `"cba"` either way,
# and the corpus reverses ASCII.
#
# NOTE FOR WHOEVER FIXES IT — there are two legitimate resolutions and they are not
# the same decision:
#   (a) make reverse() codepoint-aware, so it cannot produce invalid UTF-8; or
#   (b) declare it a BYTE operation and say so in QUICKSTART, as was done for the
#       ASCII-only is-predicates.
# What is NOT defensible is the current state: documented as "Reverse the string",
# behaving as a byte operation, and silently manufacturing invalid UTF-8. Note that
# (b) still leaves a stdlib call that turns valid text into invalid text with no
# warning, which is a poor fit for a language whose headline is contracts.
#
# The output of this probe is deliberately invalid UTF-8. That broke the runner
# until `grep -a` was added — it treated the stream as binary and DROPPED the row,
# which read as "never printed" rather than "harness ate it". See boundary_check.sh.
def main()
    var c = "世界"
    var a = "abc"

    # ASCII round-trips correctly under either implementation — the control that
    # shows reverse() is not simply broken for everything.
    print("asciiRev=[${a.reverse()}]")
    print("asciiRevValid=${a.reverse().isValidUtf8()}")

    # The bug. Valid input, invalid output.
    var rev = c.reverse()
    print("cjkOrigValid=${c.isValidUtf8()}")
    print("cjkOrigCps=${c.codePointCount()}")
    print("cjkRevValid=${rev.isValidUtf8()}")
    print("cjkRevCps=${rev.codePointCount()}")
    print("cjkRevLen=${rev.len}")

    # Two-byte Latin-1 range corrupts the same way — this is not a CJK-only or
    # 3-byte-only edge, it is every multi-byte codepoint.
    var e = "é"
    print("accentRevValid=${e.reverse().isValidUtf8()}")
